        "original_id": "4b366299-60f4-5e18-af22-a96cd317bc3f",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Pelham D. Glassford\n- Organizations: District of Columbia Police Department\n- Events: 1932 Bonus Army protests\n- Groups: Bonus Army\n- Places: Washington, D.C.\n- Concepts: service certificates, cash-payment redemption\n\nFacts:\n- In the summer of 1932, the Bonus Army gathered in Washington, D.C. to demand cash-payment redemption of service certificates.\n- The Bonus Army consisted of about 43,000 marchers, including 17,000 U.S. World War I veterans, their families, and affiliated groups.\n- Pelham D. Glassford later served as Superintendent of the District of Columbia Police Department.\n- Pelham D. Glassford was held in large measure responsible for the violence that ended the 1932 Bonus Army protests.",
